+// KV reuse past a divergence (TEN-196): smallest run worth a K-shift (llama-server's
+// --cache-reuse default is 0, its docs suggest 256; a chat turn drops one think
+// block per answer, so a short run is a coincidence), and how many splices to try.
+static constexpr size_t KV_REUSE_MIN_CHUNK = 32;
+static constexpr size_t KV_REUSE_MAX_MOVES = 8;
+// A K-shift rotates the K of EVERY cell in the buffer (graph input is sized n_ctx, not
+// the moved range), so its cost scales with n_ctx while the re-prefill it saves scales
+// with the reused tokens. Reuse only pays above n_ctx / KV_REUSE_CTX_RATIO tokens.
+// Estimate, Qwen3-1.7B (28 layers, 8 KV heads x 128 = 1024 K dims, f16):
+//   FLOPs: shift ~3 flops x 28.7k elements per cell = ~86 kflop/cell, prefill ~3.4 Gflop
+//   per token: ~40k cells per token, so FLOPs say the shift is nearly free.
+//   It is memory bound instead: 28.7k elements x 2 B x (read + write) = ~115 kB per
+//   cell, 2.7 GB at n_ctx 23552, plus the worst-case graph reserve memory_update()
+//   does afterwards. Prefill amortises the weights over a whole ubatch, so a token
+//   costs far less than its flops/bandwidth ratio suggests.
+// Measured (Dimensity 900, CPU, Qwen3-1.7B, n_ctx 23552): the shift took 2.0 s (1964 ms
+// for 688 reused tokens), prefill runs 12-16 tok/s, so break-even is about 25-30 reused
+// tokens. 368 gives n_ctx / 368 = 64 tokens there, ~2.5x margin, scaling with kv size
+// like the shift does.
+static constexpr size_t KV_REUSE_CTX_RATIO = 368;
